## Express

### Express-Guideline

- Keep `src/app.js` (or `src/app.ts`) to building the app: create it, mount middleware and routers, register the error handler last, and export it; never call `listen()` there.
- Keep `src/server.js` (or `src/server.ts`, or `bin/www`) as the only entry point: import the app, and call `listen()`.
- Keep one router per resource under `src/routes/`, and the handlers it maps to under `src/controllers/`.
- Keep business rules under `src/services/`, cross-cutting request handling under `src/middleware/`, and database queries only under `src/models/` or `src/repositories/`.
- Keep environment values and third-party client setup under `src/config/`, read once at startup.
<!-- Project-specific / Express-Guideline -->

### Express-Layout

```
├── src/
│   ├── app.js|ts                  # builds the app: middleware, routers, error handler last; exported, never listens
│   ├── server.js|ts               # entry point: imports the app and calls listen()
│   ├── config/                    # environment values and third-party client setup, read once at startup
│   ├── routes/                    # one router per resource: paths and HTTP methods to handlers
│   ├── controllers/               # handlers: validated input in, one service call, response out
│   ├── services/                  # business rules; never import express or read req/res
│   ├── middleware/                # cross-cutting request handling: authentication, validation, rate limiting, logging
│   └── models/ (or repositories/) # the only modules that query the database
<!-- Project-specific / Express-Layout -->
```

### Express-Layer Dependencies

Notes for declaring architecture layers in the `Layer Dependencies` section of `.attunic/context.md`; adjust them to the project before declaring.

- For code built on Express, this suggestion replaces the JavaScript or TypeScript one: the architecture layer names are the same, with the delivery layer split out of `adapters` as `delivery`.
- Routers, controllers, and middleware are the delivery layer: they adapt HTTP; they do not decide.
- `express`, `req`, and `res` stop at the delivery layer, and ORM or database driver types at the adapters layer; business rules receive plain values.
- Declare a repository port in the application layer, beside the service that needs it: a JSDoc `@typedef` in JavaScript, an interface in TypeScript; a module under `src/models/` or `src/repositories/` implements it.
- `src/app.*` and `src/server.*` are the composition root: they build the routers and every dependency, and pass them in; no other module builds the object graph.
- Check manually: read the imports of changed files; an inner module importing an outer one is a violation.

| Architecture layer | Typical path | Depends on | Must never contain |
|--------------------|--------------|------------|--------------------|
| domain | src/domain | itself | framework, ORM, or HTTP types |
| application | src/services | domain | database access, `express`, `req`/`res` |
| adapters | src/models, src/repositories | application (its ports), domain | business rules, `express`, `req`/`res` |
| delivery | src/routes, src/controllers, src/middleware | application, domain | business rules, database access |
| main | `src/app.*`, `src/server.*`, src/config | application, adapters, delivery, domain | business rules |
| <!-- Project-specific / Express-Layer Dependencies --> | | | |
